  <content>
  <section><emphasis><emphasis>
    <para>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</para>
  </emphasis></emphasis>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Choose the quadratic equation that represents each graph.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> <figure><media alt="&lt;p&gt;A curve in an x y plane, origin O. Horizontal axis, scale negative 6 to 5, by 1’s. Vertical axis, scale negative 4 to 4, by 1’s. A curve passes through the points negative 2 comma 2, 0 comma 0, and 2 comma 6.&lt;/p&gt;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7dc411bbf2270de91b8434022d10d52c2ef5b03d"/></media></figure></item>
  </list>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(y=x^2+x\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-x^2+2\) </item>
  <item> \(y=x^2-x\) </item>
  <item> \(y=x^2+3x\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=x^2+x\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> <figure><media alt="&lt;p&gt;A curve in an x y plane, origin O. Horizontal axis, scale negative 6 to 5, by 1’s. Vertical axis, scale negative 4 to 4, by 1’s. A curve passes through the points negative 2 comma negative 2, 0 comma 2, and 2 comma negative 2.&lt;/p&gt;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8c79633ee9db28ae376fd645f3305f7e040d6f37"/></media></figure></item>
  </list>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(y=x^2+x\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-x^2+2\) </item>
  <item> \(y=x^2-x\) </item>
  <item> \(y=x^2+3x\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=-x^2+2\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item> <figure><media alt="&lt;p&gt;A curve in an x y plane, origin O. Horizontal axis, scale negative 6 to 5, by 1’s. Vertical axis, scale negative 4 to 4, by 1’s. A curve passes through the points negative 1 comma 1, 0 comma 0, 2 comma 2.&lt;/p&gt;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7b2bcbdf48c57e9872e7e9cc5a2b0a45f04ff07e"/></media></figure></item>
  </list>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(y=x^2+x\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-x^2+2\) </item>
  <item> \(y=x^2-x\) </item>
  <item> \(y=x^2+3x\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=x^2-x\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
    <item> <figure><media alt="&lt;p&gt;A curve in an x y plane, origin O. Horizontal axis, scale negative 6 to 5, by 1’s. Vertical axis, scale negative 4 to 4, by 1’s. A curve passes through the points negative 3 comma 3, negative 1 comma negative 2, and 0 comma 0.&lt;/p&gt;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2d6d2db94cfc98fa655c905ff086edd06b44a023"/></media></figure></item>
  </list>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(y=x^2+x\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-x^2+2\) </item>
  <item> \(y=x^2-x\) </item>
  <item> \(y=x^2+3x\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=x^2+3x\)</para>
<para>Without graphing the equations, answer questions 2 – 5 to complete the table.</para>

<table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Equation</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">\(x\)-intercepts</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">\(x\)-coordinate of vertex</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(y=x^2+12x\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>2a. _____</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>2b. _____</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(y=x^2-3x\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>3a. _____</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>3b. _____</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(y=-x^2+16x\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>4a. _____</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>4b. _____</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(y=-x^2-24x\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>5a. _____</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>5b. _____</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2+12x\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> What are the \(x\)-intercepts for the equation \(y=x^2+12x\)? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \((0,0),(-12,0)\) </item>
  <item> \((0,0), (3,0)\) </item>
  <item> \((0,0), (16,0)\) </item>
  <item> \((0,0), (-24,0)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((0,0),(-12,0)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2+12x\)? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -6</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2-3x\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> What are the \(x\)-intercepts for the equation \(y=x^2-3x\)? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \((0,0),(-12,0)\) </item>
  <item> \((0,0), (3,0)\) </item>
  <item> \((0,0), (16,0)\) </item>
  <item> \((0,0), (-24,0)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((0,0), (3,0)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2-3x\)? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 1.5</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=-x^2+16x\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> What are the \(x\)-intercepts for the equation \(y=-x^2+16x\)? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \((0,0),(-12,0)\) </item>
  <item> \((0,0), (3,0)\) </item>
  <item> \((0,0), (16,0)\) </item>
  <item> \((0,0), (-24,0)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\((0,0), (16,0)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=-x^2+16x\)? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 8</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=-x^2-24x\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> What are the \(x\)-intercepts for the equation \(y=-x^2-24x\)? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \((0,0),(-12,0)\) </item>
  <item> \((0,0), (3,0)\) </item>
  <item> \((0,0), (16,0)\) </item>
  <item> \((0,0), (-24,0)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((0,0), (-24,0)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=-x^2-24x\)? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -12</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Here is a graph that represents \(y=x^2\). 
    <figure><media alt=" &lt;p&gt;A curve in an x y plane, origin O. Horizontal axis, scale negative 8 to 8, by 2’s. Vertical axis, scale negative 12 to 12, by 2’s. A curve, labeled y equals x squared, passes through the points negative 2 comma 4, 0 comma 0, and 2 comma 4.&lt;/p&gt;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/24018f3ad80b39441a79406ca4974776c1d0d321"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> Describe what would happen to the graph if the original equation were changed to \(y=x^2-6x\). </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list>
  <item> The graph would move to the right and down. </item>
  <item> The graph would move to the left and down. </item>
  <item> The graph would move to the right and up. </item>
  <item> The graph would move to the left and up. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The graph would move to the right and down.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> Predict the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph if the original equation were changed to \(y=x^2-6x\). </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \((0,0),(6,0)\) </item>
  <item> \((0,0), (0,6)\) </item>
  <item> \((0,0), (-6,0)\) </item>
  <item> \((0,0), (0,-6)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((0,0),(6,0)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item> In which quadrant is the vertex located if the original equation were changed to \(y=x^2-6x\)? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list>
  <item> First </item>
  <item> Second </item>
  <item> Third </item>
  <item> Fourth </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Fourth</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> equations whose graph opens upward.</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(y=-x^2+9x\) </item>
  <item> \(y=10x−5x^2\) </item>
  <item> \(y=(2x−1)^2\) </item>
  <item> \(y=(1−x)(2+x)\) </item>
  <item> \(y=x^2−8x−7\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answers:</emphasis> \(y=(2x−1)^2\), \(y=x^2−8x−7\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>Technology required. Use the three graphs to answer questions a–c. Then, use graphing technology to check each equation.</item>
</list>
<para>Graph A</para>
<para><figure><media alt="&quot;Parabola. Opens up. X intercepts = 0&amp;nbsp;and -7.&amp;nbsp;&quot;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4aeafc491ae29b91a96c11b8e424555b36123a02"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Graph B</para>
<para><figure><media alt="&quot;Parabola. Opens up. Vertex = 0&amp;nbsp;comma -16.&amp;nbsp;X intercepts = -4 and 4.&amp;nbsp;&quot;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1b3fec958046c05f54fe8dfa7e6bd83312caad5b"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Graph C</para>
<para><figure><media alt="&quot;Parabola. Opens up. X intercepts = -9&amp;nbsp;and 3.&amp;nbsp;&quot;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7543018bfe177a32a2e0ddd1ecea70ee4bae2dc3"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> Which graph represents the equation \(f(x)=x(x+7)\) or \(f(x)=x^2+7x\)? Then, use graphing technology to check your answer. </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list>
  <item> Graph A </item>
  <item> Graph B </item>
  <item> Graph C </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Graph A</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> Which graph represents the equation \(g(x)=(x+4)(x−4)\) or \(g(x)=x^2−16\)? Then, use graphing technology to check your answer. </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list>
  <item> Graph A </item>
  <item> Graph B </item>
  <item> Graph C </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Graph B</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item> Which graph represents the equation \(h(x)=(x−3)(x+9)\) or \(h(x)=x^2+6x−27\)? Then, use graphing technology to check your answer. </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list>
  <item> Graph A </item>
  <item> Graph B </item>
  <item> Graph C </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Graph C</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
    <item>Choose the quadratic equation written from the real solutions \(x=-4\) and \(x=-3\). </item>
  </list>
 </list> 

  <list>
    <item> \(y=x^2-7x+12\) </item>
    <item> \(y=x^2+7x+12\)</item>
    <item> \(y=x^2+7x-12\) </item>
    <item> \(y=x^2-7x-12\) </item>
  </list>
 
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=x^2+7x+12\). 
     \(x=-4\) and \(x=-3\) means that \(x+4=0\) and \(x+3=0\). \(y=(x+4)(x+3) = x^2+3x+4x+12 = x^2+7x+12.\)</para></section>
</content>
